# Idea Brief: Reliable Deferred Work5 Backlog and Complete Release-Cohort Visibility

Status: completed
Type: idea-brief
Updated: 2026-09-08
Next Action: execute PRM-0044 through CAMP-0173 and CAMP-0174 to Work5
Readiness Review: run explicitly with `ts: bs review <idea-id-or-path>`; ordinary brainstorming does not review
Produces: MAP-0032

## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Make the Hybrid release cohort a reliable deferred Work5 backlog. Work1 and Work2 may continue for
weeks without forcing Work3, Work4, or Work5 merely to preserve visibility. Every authoritative
release registration must be partitioned into one locally derived, privacy-safe operator group
before any list is bounded or transmitted. Document-backed registrations retain their Idea → Map →
PRM → Campaign rollups. Direct Work2 registrations remain document-free by design and appear in a
controlled **Direct Work2 outcomes** group rather than disappearing or being mislabeled as broken
planning.

The same projection contract should feed local status, the hosted Overview release section, and
the Work view's Awaiting Work5 filter. It is a read model over the existing authoritative cohort,
not a second queue. Bare `ts:work5` / `ts:ship` continues to select the complete accumulated cohort
and must still reject narrowing that would strand any registered owner or commit.

### Promotion

Formal readiness passed with all six gates accepted. MAP-0032 and PRM-0044 preserve those gates,
and CAMP-0173 plus CAMP-0174 materialize the implementation and cross-lane release work.

### Provisional Model

- A **registration** is one authoritative cohort requirement binding one owning outcome to one
  candidate commit. It is the audit cardinality and may legitimately exceed the commit count.
- A **candidate commit** is one distinct Git commit across all selected registrations. The same
  commit registered to multiple owners counts once here and once per owner under registrations.
- An **owning chain** is one stage- and cohort-scoped connected component derived locally from
  registered origins and a versioned allowlist of authoritative ownership/lineage relationships.
  Unknown or invalid relationships never cause silent merging.
- A **display group** is a privacy-safe projection row. A document chain normally maps one owning
  chain to one row. Direct-only owning chains may be aggregated by cohort and stage into one
  controlled Direct Work2 row whose `owning_chain_count` preserves the underlying cardinality.
- If bounding would omit rows, the final available slot becomes a controlled **Additional release
  obligations** aggregate. The complete partition is built first; bounding only changes detail,
  never totals or whether registrations are represented.

The local completeness invariant is stronger than a display count: assign every authoritative
registration an internal ordinal, require the displayed-group membership sets to be pairwise
disjoint, and require their union to equal the complete authoritative ordinal set. Report
construction fails closed if any ordinal is absent or duplicated. The hosted contract then checks
the privacy-safe arithmetic:

`registration_count == sum(group.registration_count)`

`owning_chain_count == sum(group.owning_chain_count)`

`candidate_commit_count` remains a separately computed global distinct count because one commit
may occur in more than one group and per-group distinct counts are not safely additive. The report
also carries a controlled projection-contract version, source revision/digest, and completeness
state. A truncation aggregate preserves the two additive equalities.

### Why It Matters

The current projection can report seven pending registrations while showing only one chain,
because `_release_chain_projection()` discards every origin whose path does not begin with
`sqlite/documents/`. Six direct Work2 obligations in the reproduced SMI cohort therefore remain
authoritative for Work5 selection but vanish from the visible backlog. That mismatch makes a clean
local cohort look untrustworthy and pressures the maintainer toward premature qualification and
release cycles.

The defect is architectural rather than merely cosmetic: a document inventory cannot be the
enumeration source for obligations that deliberately require no document. Trust requires one
projection boundary that starts from every cohort registration, classifies each registration, and
only then enriches the document-backed subset with document lineage.

### Desired Outcome

A maintainer can accumulate verified Work2 candidates, dogfood them, and later perform one
deliberate complete Work5 release with confidence that:

- local authority, local human-readable status, and hosted reporting agree on registrations,
  unique commits, owning chains, stages, and projection completeness;
- every valid registration appears in exactly one document-chain, Direct Work2, or bounded
  remainder group;
- document-chain rollups remain useful while direct work remains free of forced Ideas, Maps, PRMs,
  or Campaigns;
- the Overview and Work/Awaiting Work5 surfaces use the same source projection and cannot disagree
  through separate filtering logic;
- privacy remains allowlist-based and the hosted service receives no paths, prompts, source bodies,
  arbitrary outcomes, commit subjects, credentials, state tokens, or raw diagnostics;
- pending delivery, cohort corruption, base-anchor defects, and post-release closure or
  reconciliation debt are visibly distinct states; and
- Work5 cohort selection and anti-narrowing behavior remain unchanged.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Hybrid SQLite and the release cohort remain authoritative; projections never authorize release
  or mutate cohort membership.
- Do not add a manual release queue, ledger, database, synthetic document, or campaign.
- Keep the owner campaign queue separate from release obligations and do not reopen completed
  campaigns merely because Work5 remains pending.
- Direct work remains possible without any Idea, Map, PRM, Campaign, or other document.
- Hosted reporting remains advisory, privacy-bounded, read-only, per-instance, and strictly
  allowlisted. Independent instances are not merged into invented consensus.
- Do not change Work1–Work5 endpoints, force routine Work4 pushes, or force early Work5 releases.
- Do not make per-group unique-commit counts additive; cross-group commit sharing makes that
  mathematically false.
- Do not silently repair, rewrite, or rebase an active cohort's recorded base anchor.
- Do not reinterpret a valid direct origin as an unlinked/error condition. Missing, corrupt, or
  unknown origins are a separate cohort-health error class.
- This Idea does not implement, publish, deploy, increment a version, create a release, or modify
  downstream project workspaces.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Partition from all cohort registrations, then enrich document-backed components | Completeness is independent of document presence; preserves existing authority | Requires one shared grouping/projection helper rather than the current reporter-only filter | Preferred architecture |
| Keep document chains and add one Direct Work2 aggregate per cohort/stage | Strong privacy, simple UI, preserves direct chain cardinality through `owning_chain_count` | Does not expose each direct outcome separately | Preferred first release |
| Emit one pseudonymous row per direct owning chain | More granular recurrence/history without raw UUIDs | Stable digests add linkability and complexity and still reveal workload shape | Defer unless a demonstrated operator need outweighs privacy cost |
| Label direct work “Unlinked release obligations” | Makes absence of documents obvious | Incorrectly implies direct work is defective and pressures artifact creation | Reject for valid direct work; reserve an error label for invalid origins |
| Build groups from the bounded document inventory | Reuses current implementation | Cannot prove completeness and reproduces the defect | Reject |
| Bound only after building the full partition, with a final remainder aggregate | Exact totals and complete representation survive payload limits | Some older groups lose individual detail | Preferred |
| Increase or remove the 50-row limit | Preserves more detail | Payload/flooding risk and does not establish completeness | Reject as the primary fix |
| Add report-schema fields in a new schema while retaining old readers | Enforceable new invariant with honest legacy behavior | Requires client/server/UI qualification across mixed versions | Preferred compatibility path |
| Rewrite stored old reports into the new shape | Makes storage superficially uniform | Invents detail that old reporters never supplied | Reject |
| Accept fixed-width numeric release tags while preserving exact spelling | Supports existing tags such as `v00.04.11`; numeric ordering still works | These are not strict SemVer spellings; normalized duplicates can be ambiguous | Preferred direction, guarded by an ambiguity check/policy |
| Append an exact base-anchor correction record | Preserves audit history and permits guarded recovery | Requires resolver, lifecycle, and freeze-invalidation rules | Preferred over updating prior evidence in place |

## Open Questions

- Which relationship kinds constitute one owning chain in the first version? The allowlist must
  connect genuine document/outcome lineage without merging merely related work or allowing an
  unknown relation type to alter release grouping.
- Should direct-only components always aggregate into one row per cohort/stage, or is a controlled
  sub-classification (for example direct work versus release extension) useful enough to justify
  another enum?
- Should the Overview headline call `registration_count` “release obligations” while retaining
  the exact technical term in details, to reduce confusion with unique commits?
- Should a new schema expose both `awaiting_registration_count` and
  `released_pending_reconciliation_registration_count`, or rely on stage-partitioned group sums?
- How should a compact `ts: status` cap its rows while still offering a complete human-readable
  local audit—an automatic remainder row plus `ts: status release`, pagination, or both?
- What is the approved workspace policy for non-strict-SemVer fixed-width tags? If both
  `v0.4.11` and `v00.04.11` are reachable and normalize to the same numeric tuple, should the
  resolver fail for an explicit choice rather than select lexically?
- May a base correction apply only while a cohort is `working`, with frozen cohorts requiring an
  explicit recovery/re-freeze route and released cohorts remaining immutable? This is the safest
  provisional boundary but needs PRM review.
- Does an incorrect historical root anchor warrant a health finding when every candidate remains
  reachable, or only when it changes the content range or qualification subject?

## Decisions

- Start projection from the complete authoritative registration set. Document presence is
  enrichment/classification input, never an inclusion filter.
- Use **Direct Work2 outcomes** as the controlled operator label for valid document-free origins.
  Use a distinct error state for unknown, missing, corrupt, or unsupported origins.
- Preserve three different top-level counts: registrations, globally unique candidate commits,
  and distinct owning chains. Also distinguish display-row count from owning-chain count whenever
  direct or overflow aggregation occurs.
- Derive Overview and Work/Awaiting Work5 from the same reported group list. The Work view should
  render direct/overflow release-obligation rows separately from document snapshots rather than
  fabricating documents solely to satisfy its current artifact-only filter.
- Build and validate the complete partition before applying bounds. A final controlled remainder
  group keeps all registrations visibly accounted for.
- Treat a healthy `working` or `frozen` cohort as pending release, not an error. Treat invalid
  membership/reachability, missing base evidence, multiple mutable cohorts, or incomplete frozen
  state as cohort errors. Treat `released-pending-reconciliation` as closure/reconciliation debt,
  not pending publication.
- Preserve old report schemas and stored reports as versioned historical observations. Never claim
  complete grouping for a schema that did not prove the partition invariant.
- Keep Work5 selection based on authoritative cohort membership and retain strict refusal of any
  narrowed selection that strands registrations.

## Don't Forget

- Current code confirms that `SEMVER_TAG` rejects fixed-width segments, so `v00.04.11` is skipped
  and `_base_tag()` can fall back to `root:<sha>` even when that tag is reachable. The hosted
  version parser already compares digit-only segments numerically, so tag parsing is inconsistent
  across surfaces.
- Base resolution must compare numeric versions but preserve the exact Git tag. Normalized
  duplicates, missing tag objects, non-ancestor tags, and malformed policy must fail visibly.
- A guarded base-repair design should have a read-only preview plus exact apply, bind the cohort
  revision/state token and old/new tag commits, prove the new base is an ancestor of HEAD and every
  candidate, and append correction evidence instead of editing history. Preview never writes.
- A working-cohort correction must not change candidate membership. A frozen correction changes
  the qualification subject and therefore cannot silently preserve frozen evidence. A released or
  terminal cohort should remain immutable except for an explicit recorded diagnostic/recovery
  process.
- For report schemas before chain projection, show the authoritative legacy pending count with
  “grouping unavailable,” not “no chains.” For existing chain schemas that lack the new invariant,
  show “legacy/unverified grouping”; if their totals contradict one another, show an incomplete
  projection warning rather than trusting the smaller visible list.
- A new reporter schema should be additive and strictly allowlisted; the server should continue to
  accept old schemas, retain each instance's reported schema, and avoid rewriting stored reports.
  Mixed-version project views must name the source instance/schema and never merge their totals.
- The local `ts: status` / `ts: queue` capsule should show cohort lifecycle, exact base tag and
  resolved commit, projection completeness, registrations, unique commits, owning chains, stage
  counts, each controlled group, and cohort-health or reconciliation findings in separate blocks.
  It should provide a complete human-readable release audit path without requiring raw JSON.

## Compatibility And Migration Considerations

1. Introduce the new group shape under the next report schema rather than changing schema 5–9
   semantics in place. Keep legacy validation and presentation paths.
2. Compute new projections entirely from existing cohort, cycle, artifact, relationship, and
   document authority. No data migration or new release ledger should be required.
3. Treat legacy stored reports as observations with `grouping unavailable` or
   `legacy/unverified`; do not infer direct groups retroactively.
4. Upgrade local reporter and hosted contract/UI together, then qualify mixed old/new clients and
   a newer report followed by a stale older-instance report. Per-instance provenance remains
   visible.
5. Add base-tag parsing/policy independently from any cohort correction. Existing incorrect
   anchors remain explicit until a guarded preview/apply flow is approved and exercised.

## Representative Acceptance Scenario For Later Planning

A fixture has seven authoritative registrations over six unique commits: six registrations owned
by direct outcome capsules, one document-backed registration, multiple commits under one owner,
and one commit registered to multiple owners. It must prove all of the following:

- the authoritative cohort still contains seven registrations and Work5 selects all seven;
- the projection reports six globally unique candidate commits;
- every registration ordinal appears in exactly one document-chain, Direct Work2, or remainder
  group, with no duplicates, and group registration counts sum to seven;
- distinct owning-chain and displayed-group counts retain their separate meanings;
- the document-backed component retains its Idea/Map/PRM/Campaign rollup where present;
- direct outcomes are visibly represented without any path, origin UUID, prompt, source body,
  arbitrary outcome or summary, commit subject, state token, or raw diagnostic;
- Overview, Work/Awaiting Work5, reporter payload, hosted validation, and local human status show
  consistent totals and stages;
- truncation preserves complete totals and adds a remainder group rather than dropping work;
- old schemas and mixed-version instances display honest degraded coverage instead of false zero;
- fixed-width tag resolution, normalized-tag ambiguity, guarded base-repair preview, stale-token
  refusal, lifecycle restrictions, and append-only correction evidence are covered; and
- bare Work5 selection and anti-narrowing tests remain unchanged and passing.

Additional regression coverage should include a property-style partition test across randomized
owner/commit multiplicity, privacy allowlist rejection, document/direct mixed components, invalid
origin classification, more than 50 display rows, released-pending-reconciliation separation, and
UI parity between Overview, Work, and local status.

